21. Complete Lab Flow

The complete process is:

1. Create lab directory
        ↓
2. Create Python virtual environment
        ↓
3. Install Boto3
        ↓
4. Install AWS CLI
        ↓
5. Configure IAM credentials
        ↓
6. Verify AWS CLI
        ↓
7. Install amazon.aws collection
        ↓
8. Create localhost inventory
        ↓
9. Create EC2 Ansible playbook
        ↓
10. Run ansible-playbook
        ↓
11. Ansible uses amazon.aws.ec2_instance 
        ↓ 
12. AWS API creates EC2 instance 
        ↓ 
13. Verify EC2 instance

=> Key Points to Remember

a) Boto3 → Python SDK used to communicate with AWS.
b) AWS CLI → Allows us to manage AWS from the terminal.
c) IAM → Controls permissions for accessing AWS resources.
d) amazon.aws collection → Provides AWS modules for Ansible.
e) ec2_instance module → Used to create/manage EC2 instances.
f) localhost inventory → Runs the AWS automation from our local machine.
g) key_name → AWS EC2 key pair name, not the .pem filename.
h) AMI ID → Defines the operating system image.
i) region → Defines where the EC2 instance will be created.
Never upload AWS credentials to GitHub. 

=> Conclusion

In this lab, we used Ansible to automate the creation of an AWS EC2 instance.

Instead of manually creating the EC2 instance from the AWS Console, we created an Ansible playbook that communicates with AWS through the amazon.aws collection and AWS APIs.
